<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="starch degradation II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6724" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Plants accumulate and mobilize |FRAME: Starch| in both photosynthetic tissue (leaves) and non-photosynthetic storage tissues (such as tuber and seed endosperm). In leaves, starch is synthesized during the light period and is degraded during the dark period, as depicted in this pathway. In storage tissues, starch is degraded during tuber sprouting and seed germinating. Except for seed endosperm where starch degradation occurs in an acellular tissue (see |FRAME: PWY-842|), starch degradation generally occurs in the plastids (chloroplast of leaves and amyloplast of tubers) where starch accumulates. Isoforms of the starch degradation enzymes are also found and in many cases abundant in extra-plastidic subcellular locations in leaves and tubers. Their physiological functions are not well understood |CITS: [20192737]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; In leaves, starch biosynthesis leads to the formation of granules in the chloroplast stroma during the light period and the granules are degraded during the dark period. The starch granules have very complex structures. |FRAME: Starch Starch| is composed of two different polyglucan chains, |FRAME: CPD-7043 amylopectins| and |FRAME: Alpha-Amyloses &amp;alpha;-amyloses|. The |FRAME: CPD-7043 amylopectin| chains form double helices that are tightly packed in layers of crystalline lamellae. Starch degradation must proceed from the outside of these granules and requires the disruption of the tight association between amylopectin chains |CITS: [20192737]|. Recent work has helped to reveal multiple enzymes that are involved in making the exterior polyglucan chains on the starch polymers more accessible to hydrolytic degradation. Notably, in Arabidopsis two enzymes, |FRAME: AT1G10760-MONOMER| (GWD) |CITS: [16914145][17666166]| and |FRAME: AT5G26570-MONOMER| (PWD) |CITS: [15686522][15618411]| sequentially catalyze the addition of phosphate to the C6 and then the C3 residues of glucosyl residues in the amylopectin chains to move them from an insoluble to a soluble state |CITS: [19325165][20192737]|. The SEX4 |FRAME: AT3G52180-MONOMER| can help to make these soluble chains better substrates for the hydrolytic activity of &amp;beta;-amylases by removing the phosphates residues |CITS: [20192737]|. &amp;beta;-amylases such as |FRAME: AT4G17090-MONOMER BAM3| remove maltose units from the &amp;alpha;-1,4-linked unbranched chains of the amylopectin. Maltose is then transported out of the chloroplast for inclusion in various cytosolic sugar metabolism pathways |CITS: [14704427]|. As |FRAME: AT4G17090-MONOMER BAM3| and other chloroplastic &amp;beta;-amylases release maltose from the exposed chains, they eventually encounter branch points introduced by &amp;alpha;-1,6-linked glucosyl residues. Glucoses linked in this manner are not substrates for &amp;beta;-amylases so these enzymes cannot act on the exposed chains again until a debranching enzyme, such as |FRAME: AT4G09020-MONOMER isoamylase 3| (ISA3) removes the &amp;alpha;-1,6 linkages. Chloroplastic &amp;beta;-amylases can then continue to release maltose units for export. Malto-oligosaccharide chains containing fewer than four glucosyl residues can be acted on by |FRAME: AT5G64860-MONOMER DPE1| or other chloroplastic disproportionating enzymes to release glucose. This glucose can also be transported to the cytosol for use in sugar metabolism |CITS: [20192737]|. Though it is not depicted in this pathway, Chloroplastic &amp;beta;-amylases enzymes also likey act on any &amp;alpha;-1,4-linked-malto-oligosaccharide chains that are free in the stroma that contain at least four glucose residues |CITS: [20192737]|. In some species, including |FRAME: TAX-3055| |CITS: [17018036]|and |FRAME: TAX-4530| (rice) |CITS: [18621947]|, the |FRAME: Linear-Malto-Oligosaccharides malto-oligosaccharides| may also be broken down to |FRAME: GLC-1-P| by malto-oligosaccharide phosphorylase enzymes (see |FRAME: RXN-12392 reaction|). Although a chloroplastic enzyme with this activity can be found in Arabidopsis (|FRAME: AT3G29320-MONOMER PHS1|), there is no evidence that it actually participates in starch degradation in that species |CITS: [15173560]|. In general, the potential &lt;i&gt;in planta&lt;/i&gt; role of these enzymes in starch degradation remains a matter of on-going debate and investigation |CITS: [20192737]|.</Description>
